<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wallonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wallonia is the mainly French-speaking southern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, with its capital at Namur and major cities including Liège, Charleroi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, iron and glass industries made it the heart of Belgium's industrialisation in the 19th century, the first on the European continent. Industrial decline after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and linguistic tensions led to Belgium's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">federalisation</a>, and Wallonia became a self-governing region in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wallonia.jpg" alt="Wallonia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tom Lemmens · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wallonia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
